Description: Read exec and exec_always quoting like i3
 sway read the quotes and backslashes in a launch command differently
 depending on where it came from: a config line at startup kept every
 backslash, the same line on reload went through C-style unescaping,
 IPC only stripped quotes, and bindings were unescaped at load and
 quote-stripped when they fired. None of these matched i3, so a config
 shared between the two sessions, or a launcher that hands sway a
 desktop entry's command, could not start a program whose path has a
 space (exec "\"/opt/My App/run\"" works in i3 only), and the same line
 could give a program different arguments before and after a reload.
 .
 Launch commands now read their argument the way i3 does. Config lines,
 at startup and on reload alike: a double-quoted argument ends at the
 next " without a backslash in front of it, text after it is a config
 error (the command still runs), anything else is the rest of the line;
 only \" is unescaped. Runtime commands (IPC, and bindings and for_window
 rules when they fire): a backslash takes the next character inside
 double quotes, only blanks may follow the closing quote, an unquoted
 argument ends at the first ; , or line break outside quotes, and \" and
 \\ are unescaped. Bindings and rules store their launch commands after
 the config reading, so they get the runtime reading when they fire. An
 empty quoted argument is an error. sway's single-quote extension stays:
 an argument that is exactly one single-quoted string runs without those
 quotes. Every other command is parsed as before.
 .
 unescape_string() no longer reads past a trailing backslash, which it
 did for any config line whose last argument ended in one (a heap
 over-read that the binding storage could otherwise reach more often).
 .
 Regolith-only part: with 08-variable-command-name, a variable that holds
 exec or exec_always at the start of a runtime command gets the same
 launch reading.
